```java
try {
    readFile();
} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {  // most specific
    handleMissing(e);
} catch (IOException e) {             // broader I/O failures
    handleIo(e);
} catch (Exception e) {               // last-resort fallback
    handleGeneric(e);
}
```
*Subclasses are listed before their superclasses, so each block is reachable.*

```java
try {
    handleRequest();
} catch (Throwable t) {        // also catches OutOfMemoryError!
    log.error("failed", t);
    // App keeps serving requests on a corrupted heap.
}
```

```java
try {
    handleRequest();
} catch (ApplicationException e) {
    // This boundary knows how to map this application failure.
    log.error("request failed", e);
    return errorResponse();
}
// Error values are not caught by this handler.
```

Catch the narrowest useful type. A broader `catch (Exception)` may be reasonable at a final request or job boundary, but it should not pretend that every Exception is recoverable.

```java
public class CatchThrows {
    void demo() {
        try {
            throw new IOException("disk read failed");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // Wrap and rethrow as a domain exception, keeping the cause
            throw new DataAccessException("could not load data", e);
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            // NOT reached for the IOException above -- siblings don't chain
            handle(e);
        } finally {
            System.out.println("runs before DataAccessException propagates");
        }
    }
}
```
*`DataAccessException` escapes the method; the second `catch` never sees it because it came from the first `catch`, not the `try`.*

```java
int compute() {
    try {
        throw new RuntimeException("boom");  // should propagate
    } finally {
        return 42;   // SWALLOWS the exception; method returns 42
    }
}
// Caller sees 42 and never learns an exception occurred.
```

```java
int compute() {
    Resource r = open();
    try {
        return r.read();   // this value is the real result
    } finally {
        r.close();         // cleanup only -- no return, no throw
    }
}
// An exception from read() now propagates correctly to the caller.
```
*Keep `finally` to cleanup; let the `try`/`catch` own the return value and any exception.*
